# AI loop governance

These rules govern all `/ai-spec`, `/ai-build`, `/ai-review` and `/ai-status` work in this repository.

## Before loop work

- Read `AI_LOOP.md` at the repository root before any specification, implementation or review work.
- Follow the project commands and architecture documented there.
- Do not invent checks. If a command or check does not exist in the repository, do not run a substitute and claim it is equivalent.
- Missing required CI must not be treated as passing CI.

## Specification and approval

- Acceptance criteria are binding.
- Non-goals are binding.
- One approved feature issue per branch and pull request.
- Do not make unrelated improvements or opportunistic refactors.
- Do not alter product behaviour outside the approved specification.
- Stop and request human input when a genuine product decision remains unresolved.
- Only a human may approve a specification.
- After explicit human approval in the active `/ai-spec` conversation, the spec agent may create the GitHub issue and apply the `agent-ready` label.
- The agent must never apply `agent-ready` without explicit human approval of the complete specification.

## Working tree and Git safety

- A dirty working tree is a firm stop for implementation work.
- Do not offer to build around unrelated tracked or untracked files.
- Never use destructive Git operations to remove existing work.
- Never stash, reset, delete or overwrite unrelated changes.
- Never expose, print or commit secrets.
- Never merge a pull request.
- Never enable auto-merge.
- Only a human may merge.

## Autonomous build verification

- `/ai-build` may implement, run checks, use available browser tools, diagnose failures and correct its implementation autonomously.
- The builder must run every relevant command documented in `AI_LOOP.md`.
- The builder must not claim a browser test or automated check passed unless it actually ran successfully.
- Browser testing must use the applicable verification journeys from the approved issue and `AI_LOOP.md`.
- If browser tools, credentials, test data or the development environment are unavailable, record the affected verification as pending rather than pretending it passed.
- Before opening a PR, use a fresh read-only reviewer subagent when available.
- The internal reviewer must inspect the approved contract, current diff, tests and verification evidence.
- The builder may address internal must-fix findings and repeat verification.
- Allow no more than two internal reviewer correction rounds.
- After two unsuccessful correction rounds, stop and require human review.
- If fresh subagents are unavailable, do not describe the builder's own review as independent.

## Final review

- `/ai-review` must run in a separate fresh Cursor chat that did not implement the feature.
- The final reviewer must review the exact current PR commit.
- The final reviewer remains read-only.
- The final reviewer never edits, pushes, merges or enables auto-merge.

## High-risk changes

Authentication, permissions, payments, billing, database migrations, deployment, secrets, security rules, infrastructure, production data access and destructive data changes always require human review.

Project-specific high-risk areas for GigGauge (see `AI_LOOP.md`): the calculation and tax engine (`src/calculations/**`), tax rates and thresholds, tax-accuracy claims and disclaimers, and the versioned localStorage schema.
